# Rank 1747 / CVE-2020-6164: Silverstripe framework shipped four
# default top-level routes in _config/routes.yml (`interactive`,
# `InstallerTest//$Action/$ID/$OtherID`, `SapphireInfo//...`,
# `SapphireREPL//...`), reachable unauthenticated over the web. Hitting
# /interactive ran SapphireREPL::index(), whose non-CLI branch returns
# "The SilverStripe Interactive Command-line doesn't work in a web
# browser. Use 'sake interactive'..." -- the response itself is the
# payload, disclosing that the domain runs Silverstripe (CWE-200;
# GHSA-gm5x-hpmw-xpxg: "no disclosure of the specific version"; the
# REPL's eval loop is "limited to execution in a CLI context, and is
# not known to present a vulnerability through web-based access").
# Pool SHA 91d30db88f resolves only in silverstripe/silverstripe-
# framework and names this CVE in its message; the cms clone carries
# all 7047 commits and no such object (GitHub "not our ref"), and the
# advisory lists framework among the affected packages with its own
# patched versions (4.4.7, 4.5.4) -- the repo! flag is the multi-
# repository-advisory case, not a mislink. The fixed revision removes
# the four route registrations and deletes SapphireREPL.php;
# InstallerTest.php and SapphireInfo.php survive differing only by an
# added @deprecated docblock line. Remediation verified present at
# the fixed revision.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked, authorization decided by wiring/configuration,
# the family of ranks 1514, 1535, 1540, 1556, 1557 and 1593. Three
# conversion paths checked and none holds.
#
# 1. Nothing later-shipped fires. Measured on both revisions, three
#    --cache off runs per side, byte-identical within each side:
#    920 findings and 965 review flags at 91d30db88f^ against 917 and
#    963 at 91d30db88f. The whole delta is SapphireREPL.php's deletion
#    -- three VYQL-INJ-004 HtmlRender rows on the REPL's `echo
#    CLI::text(...)` colour lines (63/64/98), sourced by cross-file
#    library-param taint from FileField.php:175, and two
#    core.PathAccessCheck flags on its preg_match guards (84/85). The
#    absolute counts sit one above the first pass's 919/916 with the
#    same delta of three, so the knowledge base moved and the
#    discrimination did not.
# 2. The route file IS reachable, which corrects the first pass's
#    "the remediation lives in a file no PHP frontend reads" -- no PHP
#    frontend reads it, but the config frontend claims .yml and emits
#    per-line events (a bare `privileged: true` yaml probes as
#    VYQL-CFG-010). A throwaway line-literal binding on the Director
#    rule mapping (`config_line_contains_events` needle `':
#    SilverStripe`, emitting an already-consumed issue concept) labels
#    eight rule lines at 91d30db88f^ (_config/routes.yml:6 and
#    14-20) and still labels four at 91d30db88f (:6 and 14-17) -- the
#    four registrations the fix deliberately kept (`''`, Security,
#    CMSSecurity, dev -> DevelopmentAdmin). 8 -> 4, not 8 -> 0: the
#    binding reports the remediated configuration as vulnerable. No
#    property readable in the YAML separates the removed rules from
#    the retained ones -- same shape, same mapping syntax, same
#    SilverStripe\Dev namespace (the retained `dev` rule points there
#    too), and no Silverstripe route declaration carries an
#    authentication signal, because the framework puts authorisation
#    in the PHP controller (allowed_actions, init()) rather than in
#    the route. That is the measured-identical-on-both-revisions
#    family of 1331, and an allowlist of the framework's own
#    controllers would encode the fixing commit's knowledge.
# 3. No PHP-side discriminator exists by construction: the fix's only
#    PHP changes are the docblock lines, so InstallerTest.php and
#    SapphireInfo.php are byte-identical across the revision pair and
#    any controller-keyed signal scores the same on both. The 1380
#    counterweight fails because the fixed revision deletes the
#    guarded method rather than leaving an API call inside it. The
#    sink half still works -- `vyql match` labels `eval($command)` at
#    SapphireREPL.php:92 code.CodeEval (php.sinks@resolved) with an
#    empty source list, nothing treats fgets(STDIN) as untrusted, and
#    a STDIN source measures 72 PHP files across the sibling clones
#    with 2 eval co-occurrences, both MediaWiki maintenance REPLs that
#    are by-design CLI evaluators -- flagging them contradicts the
#    advisory's own scoping of the weakness to the web route. An
#    allowed_actions permission-gate binding measures 22 framework
#    classes on the bare-name form, all authorising elsewhere.
#    Disclosure concepts re-checked and still not covering a static
#    product-identity string: code.ErrorDetailDisclosure,
#    code.AbsolutePathDisclosure, code.VerboseHttpErrorObjectDisclosure;
#    "controller action returns a literal" fires on ordinary content
#    actions at both revisions.
#
# No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: joining a YAML route registration to the
# PHP controller that gates it needs a config-to-PHP class resolution
# no frontend provides, which is a new capability rather than a
# localized correction to a construct the engine already handles, and
# no taint path is interrupted (vyql match labels the servlet's
# request reads; nothing in the service wants for one).
#
# Spec cve_rank1747 pins the miss at both layers. The config pair
# below isolates the construct: the vulnerable route set (eight rules)
# and the fixed route set (the four retained rules) differ only in the
# four removed registrations, and no rule fires on either -- when
# default-route vocabulary lands it must separate them, which is
# exactly the property measured absent above. The PHP members pin the
# rules of the right classes that exist today: the REPL's eval loop
# against VYQL-INJ-003 (the sink is labelled; the member must flip to
# expect only if console input ever becomes core.UserControlledData,
# which the precision measurement argues against), and the routed
# disclosure actions against VYQL-SEC-004. SapphireInfo's own ADMIN
# gate is drawn from the same tree at both revisions and must stay
# reject under all of them.

test "rank1747: the shipped default route set registers four unauthenticated diagnostic routes"
  lang config
  file _config/routes.yml
  reject VYQL-SEC-004
  code
  ```
  ---
  Name: rootroutes
  ---
  SilverStripe\Control\Director:
    rules:
      '': SilverStripe\Control\Controller
  ---
  Name: coreroutes
  After:
    - '#rootroutes'
  ---
  SilverStripe\Control\Director:
    rules:
      'Security//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Security\Security
      'CMSSecurity//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Security\CMSSecurity
      'dev': SilverStripe\Dev\DevelopmentAdmin
      'interactive': SilverStripe\Dev\SapphireREPL
      'InstallerTest//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Dev\InstallerTest
      'SapphireInfo//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Dev\SapphireInfo
      'SapphireREPL//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Dev\SapphireREPL
  ```

test "rank1747: the fixed route set keeps the four registrations the remediation retained"
  lang config
  file _config/routes.yml
  reject VYQL-SEC-004
  code
  ```
  ---
  Name: rootroutes
  ---
  SilverStripe\Control\Director:
    rules:
      '': SilverStripe\Control\Controller
  ---
  Name: coreroutes
  After:
    - '#rootroutes'
  ---
  SilverStripe\Control\Director:
    rules:
      'Security//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Security\Security
      'CMSSecurity//$Action/$ID/$OtherID': SilverStripe\Security\CMSSecurity
      'dev': SilverStripe\Dev\DevelopmentAdmin
  ```

test "rank1747: the routed repl controller evals console input and answers web requests with product-identifying text"
  lang php
  file src/Dev/SapphireREPL.php
  reject VYQL-INJ-003
  code
  ```
  <?php

  namespace SilverStripe\Dev;

  use SilverStripe\Control\Controller;
  use SilverStripe\Control\Director;

  class SapphireREPL extends Controller
  {
      private static $allowed_actions = array(
          'index'
      );

      public function index()
      {
          if (!Director::is_cli()) {
              return "The SilverStripe Interactive Command-line doesn't work in a web browser."
                  . " Use 'sake interactive' from the command-line to run.";
          }

          while (true) {
              $command = trim(fgets(STDIN, 4096));

              if ($command == 'quit' || $command == '\q') {
                  break ;
              }

              if (substr($command, -1) == ';') {
                  $command = substr($command, 0, -1);
              }
              $is_print = preg_match('/^\s*print/i', $command);
              if (!$is_print) {
                  $command = "return ($command)";
              }
              $command .= ";";

              $result = eval($command);
              print_r($result);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

